<.shell title="Top bar" current={:top_bar}>
  <h1>Top bar</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Navy, with the amber rule and logo. It names the team you're on, switches teams, and holds your account menu.
  </p>

  <div class="demo bar-demo" style="padding: 0">
    <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar
      current_user={sample_user()}
      current_team={sample_team()}
      current_member={sample_member()}
      team_admin?={true}
      managed_teams={sample_managed_teams()}
      member_logins={sample_member_logins()}
      current_path="/teams/squamish/activities"
    />
  </div>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every page is in one area: a team's space, SAR Duty admin, or Account settings. The bar names it after the logo, such as "Squamish SAR". There is one space per team: your role there decides which pages you see.
    </li>
    <li>
      The team switcher sits on the name when you can go somewhere else. It lists SAR Duty admin first, for SAR Duty admins, then each team where you are a team admin or a member, A to Z. The team you're on is marked; a team opened from Admin › Teams that isn't yours marks nothing. With one place to be, the name is plain text.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your initials, top right, open your name and email, Account settings, and Log out. This menu is the same on every team: it is about your login, not the team.
    </li>
    <li>
      The area's own pages are in the <a href={page_path(:side_nav)}>side navigation</a>, not the bar, so the bar has room as teams get more pages.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>On a phone</h3>
  <p>
    Below 1024px the name sits beside the logo and ends in an ellipsis when it is too long. A Menu button holds Switch team and its rows, when there is somewhere else to go, then the area's pages in their sections, then your name and email, Account settings, and Log out. The panel opens under the amber rule, the width of the screen, with the current page marked in amber. Going to another page closes it.
  </p>
  <div class="demo phone-width phone-bar-demo" style="padding: 0">
    <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar
      current_user={sample_user()}
      current_team={sample_team()}
      current_member={sample_member()}
      team_admin?={true}
      managed_teams={sample_managed_teams()}
      member_logins={sample_member_logins()}
      current_path="/teams/squamish/activities"
    />
  </div>
  <script>
    // Open the menus, so the examples show them.
    document.querySelectorAll(".bar-demo .team-switcher, .phone-bar-demo .site-bar-phone")
      .forEach(function (menu) { menu.open = true; });
  </script>

  <h3>Logged out</h3>
  <p>
    Log in is a link in the bar, white like the Menu button, at every width. It is not a
    button: it goes to a page.
  </p>
  <div class="demo" style="padding: 0">
    <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar />
  </div>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    The layout's <code>main_nav_bar</code>, built from <code>&lt;.site_bar&gt;</code>.
    <code>Web.Navigation</code>
    decides the area, its pages, the switcher's rows, and the initials. The menus are open here so you can see them.
  </p>
</.shell>
